UMR-5T human MRI

This BIDS dataset contains MRI recordings from nine participants acquired at
5 tesla on a UIH uMR Jupiter scanner. Participants sub-01 through sub-08 form
the original eight-participant cohort. Participant sub-09 contributes an
additional checkerboard task-fMRI run and its corresponding T1-weighted image.

The dataset includes 56 NIfTI images: nine T1-weighted, seven T2-weighted,
seven FLAIR, nine resting-state BOLD runs, one checkerboard task BOLD run,
nine BOLD reverse-encoding reference images, seven diffusion-weighted runs,
and seven diffusion reverse-encoding reference images. Participant sub-01
has two resting-state BOLD runs and a T1-weighted image; sub-02 through sub-08
each have one resting-state BOLD run and all three structural contrasts.
Sub-09 has one T1-weighted image and one task BOLD run, without an accompanying
reverse-encoding reference image. Scout images, clinical reports, derived
diffusion maps, and additional TOF acquisitions are not included.
Acquisition parameters and fieldmap associations are provided in the JSON
sidecars. The eight-participant resting-state benchmark cohort is unchanged.

Checkerboard task

The sub-09 run contains 600 volumes at TR = 1 s and 1.6 mm isotropic resolution.
The documented design comprises four 150-second groups. Each group begins
with a 30-second baseline, followed by four repetitions of 12 seconds of
checkerboard stimulation and 18 seconds of fixation/rest. There are 16
stimulus blocks in total. The accompanying events.tsv specifies their nominal
onsets and durations relative to the first saved volume of the complete run.
No volumes were removed from the released image.

Event timing follows the acquisition-design record and agrees with the
historical analysis script. Individual stimulus-trigger logs were not
available for independent verification; these event times represent the
documented nominal design, not measured trial-level trigger timestamps.
Analyses that remove initial volumes must adjust event onsets consistently.

De-identification and image preparation

Acquisition dates, equipment serial identifiers and nonessential free-text
metadata have been omitted. NIfTI free-text fields and extensions have been
removed. Original DICOM files and internal participant identifiers are not
distributed.

All 23 structural images have been defaced. The FSL defacing mask was estimated
on a temporary 1 mm T1-weighted copy and transferred to the native image grid
with nearest-neighbour interpolation. For T2-weighted and FLAIR images, rigid
registration to the temporary T1-weighted copy was used only to transfer the
binary mask. The released structural images retain their original voxel grid,
affine, data type and intensity scaling; values inside the retained region are
bitwise unchanged, and masked voxels are set to zero. Temporary resampled
images, masks and registration matrices are not part of this dataset.

Orthogonal before/after images were visually checked for facial removal and
unintended brain removal. This sampled visual check is not an exhaustive
segmentation-based assessment. Functional, diffusion and reference image
voxel arrays were not resampled or otherwise preprocessed during packaging.

